// The revl stdlib Router — the load-balancer component (item 161,
// docs/distribution-model.md).
//
// Load balancing and G2 (one key → one provider → one realm) are in direct
// tension. revl resolves it with **spawn + realms + router**, NOT with N
// providers of one key in one realm (that shape is refused — it destroys the
// verifiability G2 buys). The resolution, in one picture:
//
//                        parent realm
//                     ┌───────────────────┐
//       consumers ───►│  Router provides  │   ONE provider of `worker` here,
//                     │     `worker`      │   so G2 holds downstream
//                     └─────────┬─────────┘
//         requires worker in realms("w1","w2","w3") strategy(round_robin)
//              ┌───────────┬───────────┐
//              ▼           ▼           ▼
//          realm w1    realm w2    realm w3
//         ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐   one provider of `worker`
//         │ Worker │  │ Worker │  │ Worker │   PER realm — G2 holds
//         │provides│  │provides│  │provides│   per-(key, realm)
//         └────────┘  └────────┘  └────────┘
//
// The Router `requires worker in realms(...)` (the multi-realm bind, item 162)
// and `provides worker` in the parent realm. Every consumer of `worker` sees
// exactly ONE provider — this Router — and cannot tell it is talking to a pool;
// the fan-out is the Router's private business. G2 is invoked once per boundary:
// N times inside (one provider per worker realm) and once outside (this Router
// is the sole provider in the parent realm). The pool is real; the ambiguity is
// not.
//
// RUNTIME ROUTING — where the fan-out actually happens. The multi-realm bind
// records a `routes` entry on this component's IR; item 161's py-tier reference
// driver (src/revl/run.py) reads it, resolves the N per-realm provider handles
// off the same committed-view lookup a normal `requires` uses, and forwards each
// call to a live worker by the strategy. A worker that withdraws (peer-death →
// provider-withdrawal → the R2/R3 reactive coeffect the runtime already runs)
// drops out of the live set and its calls go to the survivors — reactive
// failover with no extra machinery. See docs/distribution-model.md §3 and the
// docs note docs/router.md for the per-tier emitter follow-up.
//
// ADAPTING THIS TEMPLATE. Realms are static string literals (a config-derived
// realm would make G2 unsound), so a Router names its worker realms and its
// routed service concretely — this file is the canonical shape to copy, not a
// generic import. To use it: rename `Worker`/`worker` to your service/key, list
// your worker realms in `realms(...)`, and pick a `strategy(...)`
// (`round_robin` — the default when omitted — or `least_loaded`). Provide one
// worker per named realm (each `isolate <key> in realm("wK")`), exactly as the
// three `PoolWorker`s below.

service Worker {
  fn call(request: Str) -> Str
}

// One backend per realm. Each is an ordinary component that isolates its
// provision into its own named realm — G2 holds per (key, realm), so three
// providers of `worker` across w1/w2/w3 is the multi-tenancy feature, not a
// conflict. In a real deployment these are `spawn`ed instances and/or land on
// different hosts via the TCP+mTLS seam; here they are three static shards.
component PoolWorker1 provides worker: Worker {
  isolate worker in realm("w1")
  provide worker { fn call(request) = "w1:" + request }
}
component PoolWorker2 provides worker: Worker {
  isolate worker in realm("w2")
  provide worker { fn call(request) = "w2:" + request }
}
component PoolWorker3 provides worker: Worker {
  isolate worker in realm("w3")
  provide worker { fn call(request) = "w3:" + request }
}

// The Router: requires `worker` across the three worker realms, provides
// `worker` in the parent realm. The `provides worker: Worker` header clause is
// what the route needs — the py-tier reference driver realizes the actual
// per-realm fan-out from the `routes` IR this bind records and installs the
// `_Router` proxy as the sole provider of `worker` (a routed require has no
// single-realm provider, so an emitted body is not what routes — see
// docs/router.md). A `provide worker { … }` body here would be silently
// discarded at load, so item 449 refuses it: carry only the header clause.
component RoundRobin requires worker: Worker provides worker: Worker {
  isolate worker in realms("w1", "w2", "w3") strategy(round_robin)
}
